import glob
p = glob.glob("experiments/E259*/experiment.md")[0]
t = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
i = t.index("## 3. Summary")
new = """## 3. Summary (2026-10-30 / 31, runs E259.a0 (ablation) and E259.b0 (CPU time); VAL 873, cached raw outputs, CPU)
- **Decision by the written rule: the spur / bubble clean-up STAYS.** On all seven models (fast_recall, champion, recall, j1rc seeds 1 - 3, fast_recall_na) switching it off raises vert100 by +0.44 .. +0.73 (guard <= 0.3 fails on every model, with and without the output stage), false length by +0.002 .. +0.003 and lowers topo F1 by 0.005 - 0.006. What it buys back: Q +0.0001 .. +0.0027 and lanes found +0.017 .. +0.021 (the clean-up cuts short real lane stubs), J -0.004 .. +0.006. At a matched vertex budget (+0.5 vertices is worth about -0.005 Q at the E236 slope) the Q gain disappears: no free lunch either way.
- **Prediction** {Q -0.001, tolerance 0.003, saving 0.9 ms}: Q change +0.0016 mean (within tolerance, wrong sign); the saving could not be measured (below).
- **Timing, honestly (framework item N18):** the machine was oversubscribed during both runs (load average 4 - 15; the other chat's lane plus more). Wall-clock paired savings ranged from -6.9 to +5.6 ms (E259.a0). The CPU time of the process (`process_time`, E259.b0) did not help: it equals the wall time (the process is not descheduled; the core itself runs slower under SMT / cache contention), repetition ranges of one variant span 15 - 35 ms. Only effects of about 25 % of the assembly show above the noise: junction anchoring off saves 4.9 / 4.3 ms of 19.6 / 17.5 (positive in 10 of 10 repetitions on fast_recall and champion), fork relocation off 5.4 / 3.4 ms (positive in 8 of 10), the clean-up off nothing readable (range contains zero), the output stage dp 0.2 COSTS about 0.7 - 1.5 ms loaded (negative in 7 of 10: a python loop over polylines). Timing values are provisional until an idle window; the stage ranking is consistent with E258.
- **Consequences:** (1) no stage removal beyond E258's anchoring candidate for the j1 family; (2) the DP output stage has a real cost (about 5 % of the assembly): worth a vectorised version only if it stays in the product; (3) the framework needs a rule: no timing claim on a machine whose load average exceeds the number of physical cores; timing is requested from the owner of the machine (N1 / D19).
- **Weaknesses:** a script (no metric cards), same raw outputs only, seven models of three builders; the timing is not usable for absolute numbers.
"""
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(t[:i] + new)
